<!--
  Launcher icon, background layer. Original work, Copyright (C) 2026 Eyed.

  What used to be here was the Android Studio template: `#3DDC84` — Google's
  Android brand green — under the template's guide grid, with the bugdroid on
  top. On a phone that ships this app preinstalled, that reads as Google
  endorsement, and no licence in this repository conveys a trademark (GPLv3
  s7(e) says so outright). See docs/TRADEMARKS.md.

  Deep ink slate, and flat on purpose, for three reasons:

  * A launcher icon is a 48dp decision. At 48dp a gradient is one or two
    Just-Noticeable-Differences wide and buys nothing; at 512dp it is where
    banding shows up. Flat colour survives both.
  * It has to hold its edge against *the wallpaper*, not against a swatch.
    A near-black tile disappears on a dark wallpaper, and a bone/cream tile
    disappears on a light one; both were rendered and both failed. `#1E2434`
    is lifted far enough off black to keep a visible edge on a dark backdrop
    while still reading as ink against a light one.
  * A flat fill makes this layer trivially auditable — one path, one colour,
    no `aapt:attr` gradient for a reviewer to unpick — and lets
    tools/render-launcher-icon.py rasterise the shipped `.webp` files
    straight from this file, so the bitmaps cannot drift from the vector.

  The full 108x108 is painted, not just the 72x72 viewport: the launcher
  parallaxes the background under the foreground, and anything short of full
  bleed shows the canvas edge when it does.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FF1E2434"
        android:pathData="M0,0 L108,0 L108,108 L0,108 Z" />
</vector>
